I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction range/resolution, and the frozen analysis tools/reference index. Then I’ll establish the simplest chemically plausible phase inventory, refine it in GSAS-II, and only investigate residual peak families if the complete baseline model leaves coherent unmatched reflections.

The chemistry points first to wolframite-type MnWO₄, with Mn₃O₄ and WO₃ as the physically motivated residual phases. I’m treating that only as a starting hypothesis: the next checks are the observed peak families and exact-composition entries in the frozen COD index, including manganese–tungsten oxides that could account for any systematic residuals.

Every prominent observed line is already accounted for by the P2/c MnWO₄ family, including the diagnostic low-angle peaks at 15.66° and 18.64° and the dense 30–65° sequence. The alternative Mn₃WO₆ and precursor polymorphs predict strong peaks that are absent. I’m now moving to a one-phase full-grid Rietveld model and will use its residual—not visual peak coincidence alone—to decide whether a secondary phase is warranted.

The physically bounded one-phase refinement now reaches \(R_{wp}=5.94\%\) over all 8,301 points, versus a counting-statistical lower bound of 3.46%. It gives the ordinary ambient wolframite cell (about 4.8310 × 5.7573 × 4.9965 Å, β = 91.216°) and a plausible specimen-height shift of −440 µm. No extra Bragg family has emerged; I’m mapping the remaining residuals to distinguish profile/background effects from any genuinely missing phase before refining coordinates.

The residual map supports a real low-angle surface-roughness correction but not a second phase. After recomputing all bound-hit refinements, the stable model is \(R_{wp}=5.10\%\); a March–Dollase texture term changes Rwp by only 0.03 percentage point and refines essentially to unity, so I am excluding it as unsupported. The remaining discrepancies are intensity differences within already indexed MnWO₄ peaks, so the next step is the requested heavy-then-oxygen coordinate refinement with geometry checks and rollback if needed.

The coordinate refinement is stable from five independent small starts and remains physically ordinary: Mn–O = 2.11–2.31 Å and W–O = 1.77–2.14 Å. All starts converge to the same P2/c coordinates and \(R_{wp}\) within 0.00002 percentage point. Tests of zero offset, uniaxial strain, and preferred orientation either return to zero/unity or worsen the stable fit, so they will not be retained. I’m now cleaning the accepted model’s inactive boundary terms, running the final full-grid refinement, and exporting all deliverables directly from that project.

Completed. The pattern is single-phase wolframite MnWO₄, P2/c (No. 13), with normalized crystalline weight fraction 1.000. Final fit: Rwp 5.0003%, Rp 3.7432%, goodness-of-fit 1.4492.

